{{! Wiki sidebar. Lives as the first child of a `.container.wiki-layout` flex row, before <main>, and is
    the single source for all navigation modes (Administration > Navigation), driven by the model built
    in WikiRouting.navModel: the static curated menu (navShowStatic), the auto-built drill-down site tree
    (navShowTree), or both with a per-visitor switch (navShowToggle), plus the optional "Home" shortcut
    above them all (navShowHome). The whole aside is gated on showSidebar, so `none` mode (and empty
    menus) renders nothing — including the Home shortcut, which never conjures a sidebar of its own. The
    compact-screen show/hide is driven by the hamburger in partials/header.hbs, which finds #wikiSidebar
    and wires itself. }}
{{#showSidebar}}
<aside class="wiki-sidebar" id="wikiSidebar">
    {{#navShowToggle}}
    {{! Control row for `both` mode: the optional Home shortcut (navShowHome), then a picker for which
        pane is showing. wk-nav-browser.js remembers the choice in the wk-nav-view cookie.

        Home takes the label and the width — it's the one thing here a visitor is most likely to want,
        and a labelled target beats a mystery glyph. It wears the sidebar's own nav-link styling (same
        as the switch-less .wk-nav-home-link placement below), so it reads as somewhere you can go and
        only fills in when you're actually on /home.

        The picker beside it is a menu button whose icon mirrors the CURRENT view — the same glyph that
        view's menu item carries — with Bootstrap's caret beside it. That split is the whole point: the
        icon states which pane you're looking at, the caret states that there are others to pick. An
        earlier revision used a fixed mdi-tune-variant standing for "the control", which named the
        widget instead of the state and so told a visitor nothing; a two-chip segmented toggle stated the
        state but left both glyphs to be decoded and spent width doing it. One state-bearing glyph plus a
        caret is the smallest thing that answers both questions, and it leaves Home the most room.

        The icons are mdi-view-list-outline (curated menu) and mdi-file-tree-outline (drill-down browser)
        — both outline, matching every other glyph in this column, and deliberately NOT
        mdi-format-list-bulleted for the menu: that one is near-identical to the mdi-menu hamburger the
        compact header uses to mean "open the sidebar", which is exactly why the fixed-glyph revision
        refused to mirror the selection at all. Pick a glyph with some box structure and the objection
        goes away. Keep each menu item's icon in step with what the trigger shows for that view — the JS
        reads data-nav-icon from the item it selects, so the two can't drift.

        Menu markup follows the language switcher in partials/header.hbs — Bootstrap has no checked-item
        component, so its `.active` + aria-current is the selection signal and the trailing mdi-check is
        this app's convention on top of it. Leading icon + trailing check also keeps one glyph either
        side of the label rather than two before it.

        With Home switched off the picker is the row's only child, and the CSS lets it take the full
        width and show its label — a lone icon button floating in an empty row helps nobody. }}
    <div class="wk-nav-switch">
        {{#navShowHome}}<a class="wk-nav-home-btn{{#navHomeActive}} active{{/navHomeActive}}" href="{{navHomeUrl}}"{{#navHomeActive}} aria-current="page"{{/navHomeActive}}><i class="mdi mdi-home-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>Home</a>{{/navShowHome}}
        <div class="dropdown wk-nav-view-picker">
            <button type="button" class="wk-nav-view-btn dropdown-toggle" data-bs-toggle="dropdown" data-nav-view-trigger aria-expanded="false"
                    title="Navigation view: {{#navTreeActive}}Browse{{/navTreeActive}}{{^navTreeActive}}Main Menu{{/navTreeActive}}"
                    aria-label="Navigation view: {{#navTreeActive}}Browse{{/navTreeActive}}{{^navTreeActive}}Main Menu{{/navTreeActive}}">
                <i class="mdi mdi-{{#navTreeActive}}file-tree-outline{{/navTreeActive}}{{^navTreeActive}}view-list-outline{{/navTreeActive}}" aria-hidden="true" data-nav-view-icon></i>
                <span class="wk-nav-view-label">{{#navTreeActive}}Browse{{/navTreeActive}}{{^navTreeActive}}Main Menu{{/navTreeActive}}</span>
            </button>
            {{! -end so the menu grows leftwards from the trigger: the picker sits at the right of a
                fixed-width column whose desktop form scrolls (overflow-y:auto, which forces
                overflow-x too), so a menu opening rightwards would be the one thing here that can
                clip. }}
            <ul class="dropdown-menu dropdown-menu-end wk-nav-view-menu">
                <li><button type="button" class="dropdown-item{{#navStaticActive}} active{{/navStaticActive}}"{{#navStaticActive}} aria-current="true"{{/navStaticActive}} data-nav-view="static" data-nav-label="Main Menu" data-nav-icon="view-list-outline"><i class="mdi mdi-view-list-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>Main Menu<i class="mdi mdi-check wk-nav-view-check" aria-hidden="true"></i></button></li>
                <li><button type="button" class="dropdown-item{{#navTreeActive}} active{{/navTreeActive}}"{{#navTreeActive}} aria-current="true"{{/navTreeActive}} data-nav-view="tree" data-nav-label="Browse" data-nav-icon="file-tree-outline"><i class="mdi mdi-file-tree-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i>Browse<i class="mdi mdi-check wk-nav-view-check" aria-hidden="true"></i></button></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
    {{/navShowToggle}}
    {{^navShowToggle}}
    {{! No switch to fold it into, so the Home shortcut is a plain link in the sidebar's own item style —
        it should read as the menu's first entry, not as separate chrome. }}
    {{#navShowHome}}<a class="wk-nav-home-link{{#navHomeActive}} active{{/navHomeActive}}" href="{{navHomeUrl}}"{{#navHomeActive}} aria-current="page"{{/navHomeActive}}><i class="mdi mdi-home-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i> Home</a>{{/navShowHome}}
    {{/navShowToggle}}
    {{#navShowStatic}}
    <nav class="wk-nav-pane wk-nav-pane--static{{#navStaticActive}} is-active{{/navStaticActive}}" data-nav-view="static" aria-label="Main menu">
        {{>partials/nav-items.hbs}}
        {{#canManageNav}}<a class="wiki-nav-edit" href="{{editMenuUrl}}"><i class="mdi mdi-pencil-outline" aria-hidden="true"></i> Edit menu</a>{{/canManageNav}}
    </nav>
    {{/navShowStatic}}
    {{#navShowTree}}
    {{! Drill-down page browser: JS (wk-nav-browser.js) reads the embedded tree JSON and renders one folder
        level at a time — clicking a folder drills in, pages open, an "up" control climbs back out. }}
    <nav class="wk-nav-pane wk-nav-pane--tree{{#navTreeActive}} is-active{{/navTreeActive}}" data-nav-view="tree" data-nav-tree data-current-path="{{navTreePath}}" aria-label="Browse pages">
        <div class="wk-tree" data-tree-body></div>
        <script type="application/json" data-tree-data>{{{navTreeJson}}}</script>
    </nav>
    <script src="/static/wk-nav-browser.js?v={{assetVersion}}"></script>
    {{/navShowTree}}
</aside>
{{/showSidebar}}
